  "slug": "sql-team-size",
  "title": "Team size in an org chart",
  "kind": "code",
  "language": "sql",
  "level": 4,
  "statement": "Table employees(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, manager_id INTEGER). manager_id is the id of the employee's direct manager, or NULL for someone with no manager. There can be several people with no manager. Names are unique, every manager_id refers to an existing employee and there are no cycles. A manager's id can be larger than their report's id.\n\nFor every employee, return:\n- name\n- level: 0 for someone with no manager, 1 for their direct reports, 2 for the reports of those, and so on\n- team: how many people report to them directly or indirectly (not counting themselves)\nSort by team, largest first, then by name.\n\nOutput: columns name, level, team.\nConstraints: up to 3000 employees, chains of command up to 200 people long.\n\nExample\nemployees:\n  (1, 'Nora', NULL)\n  (2, 'Omar', 1)\n  (3, 'Paula', 1)\n  (4, 'Rania', 2)\n  (5, 'Sami', 4)\n  (6, 'Tara', NULL)\nOutput:\nNora|0|4\nOmar|1|2\nRania|2|1\nPaula|1|0\nSami|3|0\nTara|0|0\n\nHint: WITH RECURSIVE.",
  "starter_code": "-- Table: employees(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, manager_id INTEGER)  -- manager_id is NULL at the top\nSELECT\n;\n",
